; 25-break.kurt

; `break` discards the current block immediately -- no proof step, nothing
; added anywhere -- without needing to dedent past it first the way
; 14-sandbox.kurt's plain dedent did.  It's for when you already know you
; want to abandon a block right now, rather than writing something at a
; shallower indentation to trigger the close.

bool A
use A

sandbox
    use false
    break            ; abandon the sandbox here, instead of dedenting out of it

A                   ; still fine, `A` is a real axiom from outside the sandbox
; false             ; would fail: `false` only ever existed inside the sandbox

; `break` works on any open block, not just `sandbox` -- e.g. abandoning a
; `proof` you've started but don't want to finish yet also gives up the
; `show` it was trying to prove, not just the `proof` block itself:

show A implies A
proof
    break

; note that there's no `qed` above -- `break` already closed the `proof`
; block itself (and the `show` promise along with it), so there's nothing
; left to write; adding a `qed` here would be an error (nothing open to close).
